Q: How to annotate two Strings as different types &amp; throw compile error A: <p>You can not change or remove the existing method signature in Java so it is not possible to throw an error at compile time. An alternative would be to create a new type for each kind of id wrapping the string value. The equals method will return false <strong>at execution time</strong> if comparing different types.</p>
<p>Example:</p>
<pre><code> class EntryId {
    private String id;
    public EntryId(String id) { this.id = id; }
    public String getValue() { return this.id; }

}

</code></pre>
<p>You will probably need to override equals() and hashCode() to compare the same type of values correctly. There are ample examples in the Internet or [so] of how to do that. Also any other methods of comparisons that you want to use will need to be implemented. If you extract the String value to compare them directly you will lose the protection of wrapping.</p>
<p>If you use different types in parameter calling that would cause an error at compile time if you mix them.</p>
<p>Example definition of a method:</p>
<pre><code>public void updateRecord(UserId userId, EntryId entryId) { ... }
</code></pre>
